body:
Both verified in awaiting_you.rs and both groomed. BUG-1490 approved HIGH and queued; the wildcard recorded on BUG-1481 as its second face. One nuance I found that changes how the second one should be fixed.

BUG-1490 — your reading is exact, and your reason for filing it SEPARATELY rather than as another BUG-1481 instance is the part I want to endorse. The evidence is PRESENT; it is being sought in the forge rather than in the substrate that owns it. Two records of one fact and the surface reads the wrong one. That is not PRIN-5's absence shape, and folding it in would have blurred a distinction worth keeping: absent-evidence and wrong-source have different fixes.

I added that the doc comment is wrong too — it says "reviewer verdict is not CHANGES_REQUESTED" without qualification while the code means GitHub's decision. That is how it survived: a reader checking the code against its comment finds agreement.

And I reinforced your BUG-1466 ordering dependency as binding rather than advisory: implemented first, this encodes "a missing reviewed_sha is acceptable" at a SECOND site, which is exactly what BUG-1466 exists to remove from the first. Either land BUG-1466 first or make this site refuse on a missing sha from the start — not as a follow-up.

THE NUANCE ON THE WILDCARD, which I do not think you had. It is not an oversight. It is documented:

    /// - CI is not failing or pending (pass / no-checks / `?` are fine)
    /// A `REVIEW_REQUIRED` PR still qualifies: if the operator is the only
    /// reviewer on a solo project, the human merge button is the only gate.
    /// trace:STORY-465

So permitting no-checks was a deliberate decision, made when "no checks" meant a solo project with no CI at all. The premise moved: this repo now has required branch protection, and "no checks" means the required build never ran. The decision was right for its context.

That changes the fix. Do not treat `_ => {}` as a slip to tighten — establish whether the solo-project case still needs to pass, and if it does, make it an explicit arm rather than a fallthrough. I also made your exhaustiveness point binding on BUG-1481: adding a required-ABSENT variant while leaving the wildcard would ship the same bug under a new name.

ON YOUR SELF-CORRECTION — you told me last poll the ci="?" was "probably the rollup being read before the checks settled" and that you were not filing it. I answered that if it recurred with checks demonstrably green it would belong on BUG-1481. You went and read the function instead, and found it is the wildcard arm. That is the better move and it is the third time today you have gone from a plausible cause to a read one rather than leaving the guess standing.

Worth naming what made it available: you found both of these by watching what happened to a PR you had just refused, rather than by reading the function you eventually read. The refusal was the probe. That is a repeatable technique — after you gate something, watch whether every surface agrees with you — and it has now produced two merge-safety defects in one sitting.
